 "meta_desc": "Free Secondary 3 maths worksheets on indices: laws of indices, zero and negative indices, fractional indices, simplifying algebraic terms and solving index equations. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "Same base: add indices to multiply, subtract to divide, multiply for a power of a power.",
 "key_sub": "A negative index means 1 over. A fractional index means a root: take the root first.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><p>For the same base <i>a</i>:</p><ul><li><i>a</i><sup>m</sup> × <i>a</i><sup>n</sup> = <i>a</i><sup>m+n</sup> and <i>a</i><sup>m</sup> ÷ <i>a</i><sup>n</sup> = <i>a</i><sup>m−n</sup></li><li>(<i>a</i><sup>m</sup>)<sup>n</sup> = <i>a</i><sup>mn</sup> and (<i>ab</i>)<sup>n</sup> = <i>a</i><sup>n</sup><i>b</i><sup>n</sup></li><li><i>a</i><sup>0</sup> = 1 and <i>a</i><sup>−n</sup> = 1 ÷ <i>a</i><sup>n</sup></li><li><i>a</i><sup>1/n</sup> is the <i>n</i>th root of <i>a</i>, and <i>a</i><sup>m/n</sup> = (<i>a</i><sup>1/n</sup>)<sup>m</sup>. Take the root first.</li></ul><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Work out 27<sup>2/3</sup>.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>27<sup>1/3</sup> = 3 (cube root)</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>3<sup>2</sup> = 9</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 9</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Work out 16<sup>−1/2</sup>.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>16<sup>1/2</sup> = 4</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>The negative index gives 1 ÷ 4</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: <span class='fr'><span class='fn'>1</span><span class='fd'>4</span></span></p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Solve 4<sup><i>x</i></sup> = 8.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>4 = 2<sup>2</sup> and 8 = 2<sup>3</sup></p><p class='sn-ex-s'>2<sup>2<i>x</i></sup> = 2<sup>3</sup>, so 2<i>x</i> = 3</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: <i>x</i> = <span class='fr'><span class='fn'>3</span><span class='fd'>2</span></span></p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Thinking 2<sup>−3</sup> is negative. It is <span class='fr'><span class='fn'>1</span><span class='fd'>8</span></span>.</li><li>Dividing by the denominator: 64<sup>1/2</sup> is 8, not 32.</li><li>Forgetting the number in a bracket: (8<i>x</i><sup>6</sup>)<sup>1/3</sup> = 2<i>x</i><sup>2</sup>, not 8<i>x</i><sup>2</sup>.</li><li>Multiplying indices when multiplying powers: <i>a</i><sup>3</sup> × <i>a</i><sup>4</sup> = <i>a</i><sup>7</sup>.</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What does a fractional index mean?",
   "a": "An index of 1/n means the nth root. For example, 9^(1/2) = √9 = 3 and 8^(1/3) = 2. For m/n, take the root first, then raise to the power m."
  },
  {
   "q": "What does a negative index mean?",
   "a": "It means 1 over the positive power: a^(−n) = 1/a^n. For example, 2^(−3) = 1/8. It does not make the answer negative."
  },
  {
   "q": "Why is any number to the power 0 equal to 1?",
   "a": "Because a^n ÷ a^n = a^(n−n) = a^0, and any non-zero number divided by itself is 1."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do you solve an equation like 4^x = 8?",
   "a": "Write both sides as powers of the same base: 2^(2x) = 2^3. Then the indices are equal, so 2x = 3 and x = 3/2."
  }
 ],
